1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,475
In the summer of 2023, Red Lobster made a promise that sounds like a dare.

2
00:00:05,475 --> 00:00:08,761
For twenty dollars, all the shrimp you can eat.

3
00:00:08,761 --> 00:00:09,461
Every day.

4
00:00:09,461 --> 00:00:10,020
Forever.

5
00:00:10,470 --> 00:00:14,607
It lost eleven million dollars on that deal in a single quarter.

6
00:00:14,607 --> 00:00:17,257
Less than a year later, America's biggest

7
00:00:17,257 --> 00:00:20,230
seafood restaurant chain filed for bankruptcy.

8
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:22,250
So the story wrote itself.

9
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:24,727
Red Lobster was killed by its own shrimp.

10
00:00:24,727 --> 00:00:26,720
Customers ate it out of business.

11
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:29,105
But read the bankruptcy filing,

12
00:00:29,105 --> 00:00:32,600
and the shrimp shows up as the last blow, not the cause.

13
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:36,470
The chain had lost thirty percent of its customers since 2019.

14
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:41,026
It was paying around a hundred and ninety million dollars a year in rent.

15
00:00:41,026 --> 00:00:45,770
And it was losing money long before the all-you-can-eat deal went permanent.

16
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:48,639
The shrimp didn't sink Red Lobster.

17
00:00:48,639 --> 00:00:53,340
It was the last wave to hit a ship that was already taking on water.

18
00:00:53,790 --> 00:00:59,710
To see where the water came in, we have to go back to Florida, in 1968.

19
00:01:03,310 --> 00:01:06,962
On January eighteenth, 1968, Bill Darden and Charley

20
00:01:06,962 --> 00:01:10,965
Woodsby opened a seafood restaurant in Lakeland, Florida.

21
00:01:10,965 --> 00:01:14,547
Darden had opened his first restaurant at nineteen.

22
00:01:14,547 --> 00:01:16,163
This one was different.

23
00:01:16,163 --> 00:01:21,430
It sold fresh seafood, at prices a family could afford, far from the coast.

24
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:25,853
It worked so well that by 1970, with just five restaurants,

25
00:01:25,853 --> 00:01:29,759
Red Lobster was bought by General Mills, the cereal giant.

26
00:01:29,759 --> 00:01:33,260
With General Mills money behind it, the chain spread

27
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:36,560
across America through the eighties and nineties.

28
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:39,867
In 1991 came the warm garlic cheese biscuits

29
00:01:39,867 --> 00:01:42,984
that would later be called Cheddar Bay Biscuits.

30
00:01:42,984 --> 00:01:47,530
For a lot of Americans, that basket on the table was the reason to go.

31
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:51,936
In 1995, Red Lobster became the flagship of a new

32
00:01:51,936 --> 00:01:56,135
company named after its founder: Darden Restaurants.

33
00:01:56,135 --> 00:02:01,060
At its peak, there were more than seven hundred Red Lobsters.

34
00:02:01,510 --> 00:02:06,525
For a generation, it was where you went for a birthday, a graduation, a promotion.

35
00:02:06,525 --> 00:02:07,136
Not fancy.

36
00:02:07,136 --> 00:02:07,870
But a treat.

37
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:13,133
That brand was so strong that even in 2016, when Beyoncé name-checked Red

38
00:02:13,133 --> 00:02:17,880
Lobster in a song, the chain said its sales jumped thirty-three percent.

39
00:02:18,330 --> 00:02:21,897
But by then, something important had already changed.

40
00:02:21,897 --> 00:02:25,330
Red Lobster no longer owned the ground it stood on.

41
00:02:26,430 --> 00:02:30,548
By the early twenty-tens, casual dining was getting squeezed,

42
00:02:30,548 --> 00:02:33,922
and Darden's shareholders wanted Red Lobster gone.

43
00:02:33,922 --> 00:02:38,040
In May 2014, Darden agreed to sell it to Golden Gate Capital,

44
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:43,710
a private equity firm from San Francisco, for two point one billion dollars in cash.

45
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:46,167
Here's the detail that matters.

46
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:50,441
As part of the same deal, the real estate under about five hundred

47
00:02:50,441 --> 00:02:54,003
Red Lobster restaurants was sold to a property company,

48
00:02:54,003 --> 00:02:58,600
American Realty Capital Properties, for one and a half billion dollars.

49
00:02:59,050 --> 00:03:00,726
It's called a sale-leaseback.

50
00:03:00,726 --> 00:03:03,443
You sell the buildings, and you rent them back.

51
00:03:03,443 --> 00:03:06,218
The buyer got most of its money back right away.

52
00:03:06,218 --> 00:03:08,010
The restaurants got a landlord.

53
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:13,031
One economist, Eileen Appelbaum, did the math in Fortune that summer.

54
00:03:13,031 --> 00:03:16,939
Golden Gate, she wrote, had "arranged to recoup seventy-one

55
00:03:16,939 --> 00:03:20,980
percent of the total investment before it even took control."

56
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:24,545
Most of those leases ran for twenty-five years.

57
00:03:24,545 --> 00:03:28,190
And the rent went up by two percent, every single year.

58
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:34,148
Appelbaum had studied thirty restaurant buyouts from the years before.

59
00:03:34,148 --> 00:03:36,351
Ten had ended in bankruptcy.

60
00:03:36,351 --> 00:03:39,577
Her verdict on Red Lobster: "We have seen

61
00:03:39,577 --> 00:03:42,960
this movie before and it did not end well."

62
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:47,464
Two percent a year doesn't sound like much.

63
00:03:47,464 --> 00:03:48,810
But it compounds.

64
00:03:48,810 --> 00:03:55,460
From 2014 to 2023, a rent rising two percent a year goes up by about twenty percent.

65
00:03:55,910 --> 00:03:58,748
And rent doesn't care how many people walk in.

66
00:03:58,748 --> 00:04:02,451
A restaurant can cut its menu, its staff, its opening hours.

67
00:04:02,451 --> 00:04:03,870
It can't cut its lease.

68
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:07,755
By 2023, Red Lobster was paying about a hundred and

69
00:04:07,755 --> 00:04:11,459
ninety and a half million dollars in lease obligations.

70
00:04:11,459 --> 00:04:14,422
More than sixty-four million of that was for

71
00:04:14,422 --> 00:04:17,520
restaurants that weren't pulling their weight.

72
00:04:17,970 --> 00:04:20,398
Meanwhile, the people stopped coming.

73
00:04:20,398 --> 00:04:23,809
By the time it filed for bankruptcy, Red Lobster had

74
00:04:23,809 --> 00:04:27,090
about thirty percent fewer customers than in 2019.

75
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:28,803
That is the trap.

76
00:04:28,803 --> 00:04:33,111
Fixed costs going up every year, and customers going down.

77
00:04:33,111 --> 00:04:37,940
Sales did rise for a while after the pandemic, by about a quarter

78
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:42,249
from 2021 to 2023, but mostly because menu prices went up.

79
00:04:42,249 --> 00:04:47,820
Fewer people, paying more, in restaurants that cost more to rent each year.

80
00:04:48,270 --> 00:04:52,030
Then came an owner who also wanted to sell Red Lobster the shrimp.

81
00:04:53,130 --> 00:04:57,988
In 2016, Thai Union, a seafood giant from Thailand, one of the biggest

82
00:04:57,988 --> 00:05:02,984
tuna canners in the world and a major shrimp supplier, bought a minority

83
00:05:02,984 --> 00:05:07,911
stake in Red Lobster for five hundred and seventy-five million dollars.

84
00:05:07,911 --> 00:05:11,450
In 2020, a group led by Thai Union bought the rest.

85
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:13,538
Think about what that means.

86
00:05:13,538 --> 00:05:16,288
The company that owns the restaurants is also a

87
00:05:16,288 --> 00:05:18,980
company that sells seafood to the restaurants.

88
00:05:19,430 --> 00:05:22,496
Then the top job became a revolving door.

89
00:05:22,496 --> 00:05:28,029
The chief executive who arrived in 2021, Kelli Valade, left in April 2022.

90
00:05:28,029 --> 00:05:32,964
A Thai Union executive, Paul Kenny, ran the chain as interim boss.

91
00:05:32,964 --> 00:05:37,750
NPR reported that Red Lobster went a year with no permanent CEO.

92
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:43,433
According to the bankruptcy filing, Red Lobster then dropped two of its breaded

93
00:05:43,433 --> 00:05:48,533
shrimp suppliers under what the filing calls "the guise of a quality review".

94
00:05:48,533 --> 00:05:52,640
That left Thai Union with an exclusive deal, at a higher cost.

95
00:05:53,090 --> 00:05:54,429
Those are allegations.

96
00:05:54,429 --> 00:05:57,837
They come from the man who later ran the company through

97
00:05:57,837 --> 00:06:00,819
bankruptcy, and Thai Union called them meritless.

98
00:06:00,819 --> 00:06:04,410
But they set the stage for the decision everyone remembers.

99
00:06:05,510 --> 00:06:08,045
Ultimate Endless Shrimp was an old idea.

100
00:06:08,045 --> 00:06:11,532
For about twenty years, Red Lobster ran it each autumn,

101
00:06:11,532 --> 00:06:13,750
for a few weeks, to pull people in.

102
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:16,363
In 2023, it became permanent.

103
00:06:16,363 --> 00:06:18,600
Every day, for twenty dollars.

104
00:06:19,050 --> 00:06:22,001
According to the filing, it was done "despite significant

105
00:06:22,001 --> 00:06:25,210
pushback from other members of the company's management team."

106
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:27,446
And it did bring people in.

107
00:06:27,446 --> 00:06:28,835
That was the problem.

108
00:06:28,835 --> 00:06:31,945
A deal like that is meant to attract guests who

109
00:06:31,945 --> 00:06:35,054
then order drinks, desserts and pricier plates.

110
00:06:35,054 --> 00:06:37,369
For a few weeks a year, it can pay.

111
00:06:37,369 --> 00:06:41,140
As an everyday price, the numbers told a different story.

112
00:06:41,590 --> 00:06:45,240
The price went to twenty-two dollars, then twenty-five.

113
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:49,022
It still lost eleven million dollars in a single quarter.

114
00:06:49,022 --> 00:06:51,809
Some restaurants ran out, the filing says,

115
00:06:51,809 --> 00:06:55,790
"often going days or weeks without certain types of shrimp."

116
00:06:56,240 --> 00:07:00,937
Even Thai Union's own finance chief admitted it on an earnings call.

117
00:07:00,937 --> 00:07:04,321
"On this promotion, we don't earn a lot of money.

118
00:07:04,321 --> 00:07:06,600
At twenty-two dollars, we don't."

119
00:07:07,050 --> 00:07:09,616
But eleven million dollars, on its own,

120
00:07:09,616 --> 00:07:13,103
does not sink a company with hundreds of restaurants.

121
00:07:13,103 --> 00:07:17,050
It made a bad situation worse, at the worst possible moment.

122
00:07:18,150 --> 00:07:22,273
In January 2024, Thai Union announced it was getting out.

123
00:07:22,273 --> 00:07:26,324
Red Lobster's "ongoing financial requirements," it said,

124
00:07:26,324 --> 00:07:31,894
"no longer align with our capital allocation priorities." It wrote down about

125
00:07:31,894 --> 00:07:37,030
five hundred and thirty million dollars, nearly everything it had paid.

126
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:41,029
A turnaround specialist, Jonathan Tibus, was brought in.

127
00:07:41,029 --> 00:07:46,100
In his sworn declaration, he wrote: "It was immediately clear that Red Lobster's

128
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:50,600
performance was deteriorating and had been doing so for several years."

129
00:07:51,050 --> 00:07:52,900
Here is the bill he found.

130
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:56,244
A seventy-six million dollar net loss for 2023.

131
00:07:56,244 --> 00:07:59,873
About a hundred and ninety million dollars in rent.

132
00:07:59,873 --> 00:08:03,857
Thirty percent fewer customers than before the pandemic.

133
00:08:03,857 --> 00:08:07,130
And the shrimp, eleven million in one quarter.

134
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:12,245
In the days before the filing, ninety-three restaurants were shut overnight.

135
00:08:12,245 --> 00:08:16,113
Then, in May 2024, Red Lobster filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy,

136
00:08:16,113 --> 00:08:18,630
with more than a billion dollars of debt.

137
00:08:18,630 --> 00:08:22,620
About thirty-six thousand people still worked in its restaurants.

138
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:27,968
In September 2024, Red Lobster came out of bankruptcy,

139
00:08:27,968 --> 00:08:32,295
owned by its lenders, led by Fortress Investment Group.

140
00:08:32,295 --> 00:08:36,857
The new chief executive, Damola Adamolekun, came from P.F.

141
00:08:36,857 --> 00:08:37,487
Chang's.

142
00:08:37,487 --> 00:08:42,600
Born in 1989, he became the youngest boss in the chain's history.

143
00:08:43,050 --> 00:08:44,543
He didn't sugarcoat it.

144
00:08:44,543 --> 00:08:47,983
"We inherited a very damaged brand," he told Fortune.

145
00:08:47,983 --> 00:08:53,370
He also said endless shrimp would never come back, "because I know how to do math."

146
00:08:53,820 --> 00:08:55,615
There were signs of life.

147
00:08:55,615 --> 00:08:59,349
Traffic rose six and a half percent in October 2025.

148
00:08:59,349 --> 00:09:02,580
Sales were up about ten percent a year later.

149
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:04,833
But the chain is smaller now.

150
00:09:04,833 --> 00:09:09,497
More than a hundred and sixty restaurants have closed since the bankruptcy,

151
00:09:09,497 --> 00:09:13,477
including Times Square, and about five hundred and forty remain.

152
00:09:13,477 --> 00:09:18,390
And in April 2026, endless shrimp came back after all, as a limited-time offer.

153
00:09:18,840 --> 00:09:21,807
The fight over what went wrong isn't over.

154
00:09:21,807 --> 00:09:27,036
In May 2026, a trust representing shareholders sued Thai Union in Florida,

155
00:09:27,036 --> 00:09:31,134
claiming it used its control to "squeeze out every drop of

156
00:09:31,134 --> 00:09:35,373
value that it could." Thai Union did not immediately respond

157
00:09:35,373 --> 00:09:39,400
to Fortune, and the claims have not been tested in court.

158
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:42,260
So, let's put the timeline back together.

159
00:09:42,710 --> 00:09:47,674
In 2014, the buildings were sold, and the restaurants became tenants.

160
00:09:47,674 --> 00:09:51,630
The rent rose every year, while customers drifted away.

161
00:09:51,630 --> 00:09:54,867
An owner that also sold seafood took control.

162
00:09:54,867 --> 00:10:00,550
And only then, with the margin already gone, came twenty-dollar endless shrimp.

163
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,620
The shrimp is the part we remember because it's funny.

164
00:10:04,620 --> 00:10:07,368
A chain eaten alive by its own customers.

165
00:10:07,368 --> 00:10:12,731
But the less funny part is what killed it: a business that had to pay more every

166
00:10:12,731 --> 00:10:17,960
year, for buildings it used to own, with fewer people coming through the door.

167
00:10:18,410 --> 00:10:21,434
Its new boss says this could be "the greatest comeback

168
00:10:21,434 --> 00:10:24,234
in the history of the restaurant industry." Maybe.

169
00:10:24,234 --> 00:10:28,490
But it starts with far fewer restaurants than the seven hundred it once had.

170
00:10:28,940 --> 00:10:32,096
If you want to see how a price on a menu really gets built,

171
00:10:32,096 --> 00:10:35,894
our video on who got paid for six-dollar eggs is a good place to start.

172
00:10:35,894 --> 00:10:36,910
I'm The Cost Curve.

173
00:10:36,910 --> 00:10:37,980
Thanks for watching.
